  "researchTitle": "Research",
  "researchSubtitle": "Four landmark findings, each with its limits and sources \u2014 plus two short experiments you can run on this device to see how the camera proxy behaves.",
  "researchHonestNote": "Nothing here measures attention, calm, or any medical state. The experiments describe a camera signal only.",
  "researchMilestonesTitle": "Landmark findings",
  "researchExperimentsTitle": "Self-run experiments",
  "researchLimitLabel": "Limit",
  "researchSourceLabel": "Source",
  "researchOpenSource": "Open source",
  "researchStartButton": "Start experiment",
  "researchStopButton": "Stop",
  "researchResultTitle": "Experiment result",
  "researchResultAverage": "Average proxy score",
  "researchResultStability": "Average stability",
  "researchResultLighting": "Average lighting",
  "researchResultPresence": "Average presence",
  "researchResultNote": "This describes the camera signal during those 30 seconds only. It is not evidence about attention, calm, or any mental state.",
  "researchRunAgain": "Run again",
  "researchBackToList": "Back to list",
  "researchMilestone1Title": "1929 \u2014 The first human EEG",
  "researchMilestone1Body": "Hans Berger in Jena, Germany, recorded electrical activity from the human scalp and described the alpha rhythm (roughly 8\u201313 Hz, strongest at rest with eyes closed). It was the start of electroencephalography \u2014 and of every non-invasive brain recording that followed.",
  "researchMilestone1Limit": "Crude early equipment and a handful of first subjects; the finding was a beginning, not a validated clinical tool.",
  "researchMilestone1Source": "Berger, H. (1929). \u00dcber das Elektrenkephalogramm des Menschen. Archiv f\u00fcr Psychiatrie und Nervenkrankheiten.",
  "researchMilestone2Title": "1988 \u2014 Spelling with the P300 wave",
  "researchMilestone2Body": "Farwell and Donchin showed that a person can pick letters on a screen by attention alone: the brain produces a P300 response about 300 ms after a rare, attended stimulus, and a computer can decode which letter was chosen. It was one of the first non-invasive BCIs with a practical use \u2014 assisted communication.",
  "researchMilestone2Limit": "Slow (a few characters per minute at the time), needs per-person calibration and training; it decodes a choice among shown options, not free-form thought.",
  "researchMilestone2Source": "Farwell, L. A., & Donchin, E. (1988). Talking off the top of your head. Electroencephalography and Clinical Neurophysiology.",
  "researchMilestone3Title": "2004 \u2014 An open motor-imagery EEG dataset",
  "researchMilestone3Body": "The EEG Motor Movement/Imagery Dataset on PhysioNet recorded 109 people performing and imagining hand and foot movements (64 channels). It became an open standard for building and comparing decoders \u2014 Nemro's Lab replays three excerpts from it (S001, S002, and S003).",
  "researchMilestone3Limit": "Lab data from healthy volunteers following cues \u2014 far from everyday use; accuracy on this set says nothing about clinical effectiveness.",
  "researchMilestone3Source": "PhysioNet EEG Motor Movement/Imagery Dataset (EEGMMIDB); Schalk et al., BCI2000 (2004).",
  "researchMilestone4Title": "2010 \u2014 Not everyone can drive a BCI",
  "researchMilestone4Body": "Work from the Berlin BCI group found that a substantial minority of users \u2014 often cited around 15\u201330% \u2014 never gain reliable control of a motor-imagery BCI despite training, a phenomenon called 'BCI illiteracy'. BCI performance depends on the person, not just the technology.",
  "researchMilestone4Limit": "The share depends on how 'control' is defined, the task, and training length; it does not mean those people's brains are in any way deficient.",
  "researchMilestone4Source": "Blankertz, B., et al. (2010). Neurophysiological predictor of SMR-based BCI performance. NeuroImage.",
  "researchExperimentStillTitle": "Sit still for 30 seconds",
  "researchExperimentStillBody": "Sit in front of the camera and stay as still as you can for 30 seconds. Watch what happens to the stability component and the score.",
  "researchExperimentStillReading": "If you sat still, stability usually rises and the score rises with it. That shows the score reacts to movement \u2014 not to focus, calm, or effort.",
  "researchExperimentCoverTitle": "Cover the lens, then uncover it",
  "researchExperimentCoverBody": "Start the run, cover the camera with your hand for a few seconds, then uncover it. Watch what happens to the presence component and the score.",
  "researchExperimentCoverReading": "Covering the lens collapses central contrast, so presence and the score fall with it \u2014 no change in mental state needed. The score follows the image it is given.",
  "labDecoderModelLabel": "Decoder",
  "labDecoderModelLogistic": "Logistic",
  "labDecoderModelCentroid": "Centroid",
  "labDecoderModelCentroidLine": "Nearest centroid \u00b7 mu/beta power \u00b7 {count} channels",
  "labDecoderTrainingInSample": "Trained on the replay itself ({train}); no held-out run.",
  "labDecoderAccuracyInSample": "In-sample accuracy: {percent}% (chance 50%)",
  "labDecoderInSampleNote": "In-sample accuracy flatters the model: it was tested on the very windows it learned from, so it cannot be compared with held-out accuracy.",
  "algorithmsTitle": "Algorithms that build AI",
  "algorithmsSubtitle": "Four algorithms, from 1847 to 2017, that turn data into a learning machine \u2014 plus three hands-on experiments that run real training on this device.",
  "algorithmsHonestNote": "Everything here runs on-device with tiny toy data. These demos teach how the algorithms work; they are not powerful models and they prove nothing about the brain.",
  "algorithmsCardsTitle": "The algorithms",
  "algorithmsExperimentsTitle": "Try the algorithms",
  "algorithmsCard1Title": "1847 \u2014 Gradient descent",
  "algorithmsCard1Body": "To teach a model, first define how wrong it is (a loss), then walk downhill: nudge every parameter a little against the slope of the loss, and repeat. Cauchy described the idea in 1847; it still trains nearly every model today.",
  "algorithmsCard1Limit": "It finds a downhill step, not the best answer: it can stall in a shallow valley or bounce forever if the step is too big.",
  "algorithmsCard1Source": "Cauchy, A. (1847). M\u00e9thode g\u00e9n\u00e9rale pour la r\u00e9solution des syst\u00e8mes d'\u00e9quations simultan\u00e9es.",
  "algorithmsCard2Title": "1958 \u2014 The perceptron",
  "algorithmsCard2Body": "Rosenblatt's perceptron is the first machine that learns from examples: it draws a line between two classes and moves the line a little every time it misclassifies a point, until the line separates them \u2014 or it proves they cannot be separated.",
  "algorithmsCard2Limit": "A single line can only separate linearly separable classes. It cannot learn XOR, which is why networks needed hidden layers.",
  "algorithmsCard2Source": "Rosenblatt, F. (1958). The perceptron: A probabilistic model for information storage and organization in the brain.",
  "algorithmsCard3Title": "1986 \u2014 Backpropagation",
  "algorithmsCard3Body": "Backpropagation answers 'who is to blame?' for a wrong answer: it sends the error backwards through the network, layer by layer, so every weight learns how much it contributed \u2014 and gradient descent then corrects each one.",
  "algorithmsCard3Limit": "It needs labelled examples and differentiable math; it explains how weights move, not what the network 'understands'.",
  "algorithmsCard3Source": "Rumelhart, D. E., Hinton, G. E., & Williams, R. J. (1986). Learning representations by back-propagating errors. Nature.",
  "algorithmsCard4Title": "2017 \u2014 Attention and the transformer",
  "algorithmsCard4Body": "Instead of reading words one by one, attention lets every word weigh every other word at once, so context shapes meaning. The transformer stacked attention into the architecture behind today's language models.",
  "algorithmsCard4Limit": "Attention is weighted averaging, not comprehension: fluent text is not evidence of understanding, and its cost grows with sequence length.",
  "algorithmsCard4Source": "Vaswani, A., et al. (2017). Attention is all you need. Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems.",
  "algorithmsExperimentPerceptronTitle": "Teach a line to separate two classes",
  "algorithmsExperimentPerceptronBody": "Tap the board to place points: red for class A, blue for class B. Then press Train and watch the line move until it separates them \u2014 or fails, if you placed them so no line can.",
  "algorithmsExperimentPerceptronReading": "The line only moves when it makes a mistake. If your points overlap, no line can separate them: that is the perceptron's limit, not a bug.",
  "algorithmsExperimentGradientTitle": "Walk downhill without falling off",
  "algorithmsExperimentGradientBody": "Move the learning-rate slider, then press Run. A small step crawls downhill; a huge step bounces out of the valley. The algorithm is the same \u2014 only the step size changes.",
  "algorithmsExperimentGradientReading": "Step size is the whole game: too small wastes time, too big diverges. Tuning it is most of practical training.",
  "algorithmsExperimentOverfitTitle": "Fit the noise, miss the pattern",
  "algorithmsExperimentOverfitBody": "Slide the degree from 1 to 9 and watch the curve. A straight line underfits; a wiggly degree-9 curve memorises every noisy point and will fail on new data.",
  "algorithmsExperimentOverfitReading": "Training error always falls as the curve gets wigglier \u2014 that is why it cannot be trusted alone. Generalisation, not memorisation, is the goal.",
